-- Q10740: Partnerships [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 736, payments to a retiring general partner are classified as 736(b) property payments or 736(a) ordinary income. For general partnerships, goodwill not specified in the agreement is excluded from 736(b). The $100,000 of property payments (excluding $20,000 unspecified goodwill) is 736(b), while $20,000 goodwill plus $60,000 for services equals $80,000 of 736(a).
Wrong (B): This incorrectly treats the $20,000 of unspecified goodwill as a property payment under 736(b). Unspecified goodwill is 736(a) income for general partners.
Wrong (C): The $60,000 for past services is clearly 736(a) ordinary income, not a property payment. Not all payments relate to partnership property.
Wrong (D): There is no separate capital gain category. The $20,000 goodwill is part of 736(a), not a distinct classification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10740;

-- Q10741: Partnerships [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sections 704(d), 465, and 469, partnership losses face three sequential limitations. First, $100,000 is limited to Mercer''s $90,000 basis, suspending $10,000. The $90,000 is then limited to the $70,000 at-risk amount, suspending $20,000. Finally, the $70,000 is suspended as a passive loss because Mercer does not materially participate.
Wrong (A): This correctly applies the basis and at-risk limits but incorrectly allows the $70,000 as a deduction against ordinary income despite Mercer''s non-material participation.
Wrong (C): This applies only the basis limitation and ignores the at-risk and passive activity rules, which are separate sequential limits.
Wrong (D): This skips the at-risk limitation entirely. The $90,000 passing the basis test must still pass the at-risk test before reaching the passive activity rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10741;

