-- Q10753: Partnerships [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 704(c)(1)(B), if contributed property with built-in gain is distributed to another partner within seven years, the contributing partner recognizes the remaining built-in gain ($120,000 FMV minus $50,000 basis = $70,000). This anti-abuse rule prevents shifting pre-contribution gain through distributions.
Wrong (A): Section 704(c)(1)(B) overrides the general nonrecognition rule for distributions when contributed property is distributed to a non-contributing partner within seven years.
Wrong (B): The gain is recognized by the contributing partner (Whitfield), not the distributee (Yates). Section 704(c)(1)(B) assigns the built-in gain back to the contributor.
Wrong (C): Whitfield recognizes the full $70,000 built-in gain, not a pro rata one-third share. Section 704(c)(1)(B) requires recognition of the entire remaining built-in gain.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10753;

-- Q10754: Partnerships [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 706(d)(1), when a partner''s interest varies during the year, income must be allocated considering the varying interests. Using the proration method, Chen''s share is $180,000 x 1/3 x 6/12 = $30,000 for the half-year she was a partner.
Wrong (B): This allocates a full year''s one-third share ($60,000) despite Chen joining mid-year. The proration method accounts for the partial-year membership.
Wrong (C): This allocates half the total income without regard to Chen''s one-third partnership percentage.
Wrong (D): This applies the one-third share to a nine-month period rather than the correct six months from July 1 through December 31.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10754;

-- Q10755: Partnerships [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 705(a), outside basis is increased by all income items including tax-exempt income ($5,000) and decreased by losses ($10,000) and nondeductible noncapital expenditures ($3,000). Basis = $100,000 + $40,000 + $5,000 - $10,000 - $3,000 = $132,000.
Wrong (A): Tax-exempt income increases basis and nondeductible fines decrease basis under Section 705. Neither is excluded from the computation.
Wrong (C): Tax-exempt income does increase basis under Section 705(a)(1)(B) to preserve its tax-free character when the interest is later sold.
Wrong (D): Nondeductible fines reduce basis under Section 705(a)(2)(B) as nondeductible noncapital expenditures.$EXPL$
